nsertion", "citation": null}, "anchor": {"score": {"grounding_score": 0.96, "extraction_score": 0.96}, "value": "views from the beginning,", "citation": {"bbox": [457, 773, 609, 792], "page": 1, "page_width": 633.3599853515625, "page_height": 840.0}}, "needs_review": {"score": {"grounding_score": 0.14, "extraction_score": 0.9}, "value": false, "citation": null}, "printed_text": {"score": {"grounding_score": 0.9, "extraction_score": 0.9}, "value": "", "citation": null}, "handwritten_text": {"score": {"grounding_score": 0.95, "extraction_score": 0.94}, "value": "1. Ellis Gray Loring.", "citation": {"bbox": [105, 788, 333, 819], "page": 1, "page_width": 633.3599853515625, "page_height": 840.0}}}], "citation": null}, "redline": {"score": {"grounding_score": 0.16, "extraction_score": 0.14}, "value": "{++Faneuil Hall\nmeeting Jan 8.++}\n\nSaturday Morning, {++[++}December 23, 1837.{++]++}\n\nMy Dear Sir,\n\nI am sorry to see that a meeting is advertised for tomorrow {>>underlined<<}\nevening.  I fear that the religiously disposed will not go out on\nSunday evening.  I beg, that as far as possible, all {~~preparations~>passionateness~~} may\nbe avoided.  I wish that you and Mr. Loring {++1++} would state distinctly\nthe objects of the meeting which was petitioned for, that it was intend-\ned to be held under the influence of religion, that it was to begin and\nto close with supplication for God's blessing, that it was intended\nalso to embrace all parties, that the Mayor was to be requested to pre-\nside (Am I not right in this?), etc.  My earnest desire is that we\nmay have a meeting which will unite {>>underlined<<} the people of our city in the\nexpression of the great principles in which they really concur, and\nfor this end exasperating views of one or another party should {~~con-\nsistently be~>be conscientiously~~} kept out of sight.  If the Abolitionists will now take\na generous position, if they will come forward as advocates of free\n{~~discvssion~>discussion~~}, and as the settled foes of violence and {~~mobs~>mob~~}, and will\nstop {>>underlined<<} here, they will not only acquire new confidence, but what is more\nimportant, will give a decided impulse to the great principles on\nwhich all our reformations and improvements must rest.  If they try\nto mix up {~~this~>the~~} meeting with Abolitionism, they will prevent a deep and\n{++cordial testimony++} against violence, and in favor of the rights of free\n{~~discvssion~>discussion~~} and the press.  I wish of course to be kept out of sight\nas much as I can be.  My present notoriety is painful enough.  But if\nit is necessary, you can bear testimony to my views from the beginning,\n\n{++1. Ellis Gray Loring.++}", "citation": {"bbox": [46, 19, 611, 819], "page": 1, "page_width": 633.3599853515625, "page_height": 840.0}}}, "min_confidence_score": 0.14}]]